          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For polymeric jointing sand at a standard 1/4-inch joint width and 1.5-inch depth, a 50-lb bag covers roughly 40-50 square feet, so most residential patios need one bag per 40-50 square feet plus about 15% extra for compaction settling and waste. Wider or deeper joints reduce coverage significantly — a 1-inch-wide flagstone joint can drop coverage to under 15 square feet per bag. For paver base/bedding sand at a standard 1-inch compacted layer, plan on roughly 0.5 cubic feet, or about one 50-lb bag, per 12 square feet.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between paver sand and polymeric sand?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "\"Paver sand\" is a broad term that can refer to either bedding sand (the leveling layer pavers rest on, usually coarse concrete sand) or jointing sand (the material swept into the gaps between pavers). Polymeric sand is a specific type of jointing sand blended with a polymer binder that hardens when misted with water, locking joints in place and resisting weeds and washout. Plain bedding sand is never used in the joints of a finished installation, and polymeric sand should never be used as the base layer under pavers — they serve entirely different structural roles.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is polymeric sand made of?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Polymeric sand is a blend of graded silica sand and a dry polymer binder, typically an acrylic or cross-linked polymer compound. When the swept-in sand is misted with water, the polymer dissolves briefly, coats the sand grains, and then cures into a semi-rigid matrix that binds the joint together — unlike plain sand, which has no binding agent and simply sits loose in the gap.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I use regular sand or play sand instead of paver sand?",
