            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "You can use regular, unpolymerized sand for paver joints, but it isn't recommended for anything but a temporary or budget installation, since it washes out under rain and foot traffic within a season or two. Play sand specifically is the wrong choice — it's washed and rounded for softness and safety, not compaction, so it has poor angularity and won't lock together the way angular paver bedding sand or concrete sand does. Use a jointing-specific or polymeric sand for joints, and a coarse, angular concrete/bedding sand for the base layer.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Where can I buy polymeric sand?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Polymeric sand is widely stocked at Home Depot, Lowe's, and Menards in the US, at Bunnings Warehouse in Australia, and at most independent landscape supply and masonry yards. Big-box stores typically carry mid-range brands like Sakrete and Quikrete on the shelf, while premium brands such as Techniseal, Alliance Gator Maxx, and SEK Surebond are more often found through professional hardscape suppliers or ordered online.",
