          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What sand should I use to lay pavers?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Use coarse, angular concrete sand (sometimes called bedding sand or paving sand) as the 1-inch leveling layer that pavers are set into — never use fine play sand or beach sand for this layer, since rounded grains don't compact or lock together. After the pavers are laid and leveled, fill the joints between them with polymeric sand, which is specifically formulated to bind, resist weeds, and prevent washout.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does joint width and depth really change how much sand I need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, significantly. Coverage per bag scales roughly with the total volume of the joints, which is a function of both width and depth. Doubling either the joint width or the joint depth roughly halves the square footage a single bag will cover. This is why a wide-joint flagstone patio can require two to three times more polymeric sand per square foot than a standard concrete paver patio with narrow 1/8-inch joints.",
