          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How to Measure Your Project Before You Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Getting an accurate bag count starts with accurate measurements, and the two numbers that matter most — joint width and joint depth — are the ones DIYers most often guess at instead of measure. Here's the process we use before every project.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Measure Total Square Footage</h3>
          <p>
            For a rectangular patio or walkway, multiply length by width in feet. For an irregular shape, break the area into rectangles and triangles, calculate each separately, and add them together, or measure the longest length and widest point and treat it as a rectangle — you'll overestimate slightly, which is safer than running short mid-project. If you're working on multiple disconnected areas (a patio plus a walkway, for example), measure and calculate each one separately, then add the totals before ordering.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Measure Joint Width With a Ruler, Not a Guess</h3>
          <p>
            Joint width has an outsized effect on how much jointing sand you'll need, because coverage scales with the volume of the joint, not just the surface area of the patio. Measure at three or four different spots across the installation, since paver spacing often varies slightly across a large job, and use the widest measurement you find rather than an average — it's better to have a little extra sand than to run short on the widest section.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Measure Joint Depth (or Paver Thickness)</h3>
          <p>
            Joint depth is usually close to the thickness of your paver, typically 1.5 to 2.375 inches for standard concrete pavers. If you can see down into an empty joint before sanding, measure it directly with a small ruler or tape measure. If the pavers are already installed with old sand in the joints that you're replacing, measure the paver thickness from a spare or broken piece as a proxy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: For Bedding Sand, Confirm Your Compacted Depth</h3>
          <p>
            Paver base/bedding sand is typically screeded to a 1-inch depth before pavers are set into it, regardless of paver size — this is a near-universal industry standard, not something you need to measure on an existing patio. If your project also requires a compacted gravel base beneath the sand (recommended for driveways and heavy-traffic areas), calculate that separately using a gravel calculator, since gravel and sand have different weights and coverage rates.
          </p>
          <p>
            It's also worth deciding upfront whether you're buying bagged sand or ordering bulk. Bagged sand is easier to transport in a regular car, easier to store leftover material from, and there's no minimum order — but it costs meaningfully more per pound once you're past a couple dozen bags. Bulk sand, sold by the cubic yard or by the ton, needs a truck or trailer with a tarp (or paid delivery), and most suppliers have a minimum order of half a yard to a full yard. As a rough cutoff, projects under about 15-20 bags are usually cheaper and more convenient in bags; anything larger almost always favors bulk delivery once you factor in the extra trips a car-load purchase would require.
          </p>

