        <!-- SECTION 3: Sand Types -->
        <div id="sand-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Sand Type Do You Actually Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            "Sand" is not a single product, and the biggest source of failed paver projects isn't a bad calculation — it's using the wrong type of sand for the job. Here's how the main categories differ and where each one belongs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Polymeric Sand (Jointing Sand)</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>What it is:</strong> Fine sand blended with a polymer binder that hardens when misted with water. <strong>Where it goes:</strong> Swept into the finished gaps between pavers, never underneath them. <strong>Why it matters:</strong> It's the only jointing material that resists weed growth, ant tunneling, and washout from rain — plain sand in the same joints will wash out within a season or two. This is what most people mean when they search "what is polymeric sand" or "polymeric sand for pavers," and it's the sand type our jointing-sand calculator mode above is built around.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Paving Sand / Bedding Sand</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>What it is:</strong> Coarse, angular, washed sand — sometimes labeled "paving sand," "bedding sand," or simply "coarse sand." <strong>Where it goes:</strong> Screeded into a level 1-inch layer that pavers are set directly into during installation. <strong>Why it matters:</strong> Its angular grain shape locks together under compaction to create a stable, level surface, unlike rounded sands that shift and settle unevenly. This is the sand our bedding-sand calculator mode above is built around.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Concrete Sand</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>What it is:</strong> A coarse, washed, ASTM C-33-graded sand originally formulated as a concrete aggregate. <strong>Where it goes:</strong> Frequently used interchangeably with bedding/paving sand for the base layer under pavers, since the grading requirements overlap closely. <strong>Why it matters:</strong> If a supplier doesn't stock a product specifically labeled "paver base sand," concrete sand is the standard substitute — just confirm it's washed and free of clay or organic material before buying.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Washed Sand / Fine Sand</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>What it is:</strong> Generic terms covering a range of sands that have been rinsed of silt, clay, and organic debris (washed) or that have a smaller grain size (fine). <strong>Where it goes:</strong> Depends entirely on the specific product — fine washed sand can work for leveling small stepping-stone gaps, but it is not a substitute for angular bedding sand under a full paver installation, and it is not a substitute for polymer-bound jointing sand either. <strong>Why it matters:</strong> These labels alone don't guarantee suitability for hardscaping — always check the bag for an explicit "paver," "hardscape," or "ASTM C-33" designation before buying based on the word "fine" or "washed" alone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Play Sand</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>What it is:</strong> Extremely fine, rounded, washed sand sold for sandboxes, sold specifically for softness and safety around children. <strong>Where it goes:</strong> Sandboxes only. <strong>Why it matters:</strong> Its rounded grain shape is the opposite of what compaction requires — it won't lock together as a stable paver base, and it won't hold in joints the way angular jointing sand does. If you've landed here searching something like "magic sand" or a sandbox-play-sand product, that's a different category of product entirely from anything used in a hardscape installation, and none of the coverage math on this page applies to it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
