                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~6 sq ft</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            For paver base sand, coverage is simpler because it doesn't depend on joint geometry: a 50-lb bag spread at the standard 1-inch depth covers roughly 12 square feet. A 1,000-square-foot driveway base, for comparison, would need over 80 bags — well past the point where ordering bulk sand by the ton from a landscape supplier is dramatically cheaper than buying individual bags at a home improvement store.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Where to Buy -->
        <div id="where-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Where to Buy Paver Sand
          </h2>
          <p>
            In the United States, <strong>Home Depot, Lowe's, and Menards</strong> all stock polymeric sand and bedding sand year-round in most regions, typically carrying Sakrete, Quikrete, and store-brand options on the shelf alongside a smaller selection of premium brands like Techniseal. Big-box stores are the fastest option for a same-day small or mid-size project, but their bulk-order and delivery options for large volumes are usually more limited than a dedicated supplier's.
          </p>
          <p>
            In Australia and New Zealand, <strong>Bunnings Warehouse</strong> is the equivalent go-to retailer, stocking bagged washed sand, paving sand, and polymeric jointing products, along with bulk bags for larger projects. As with US big-box stores, Bunnings is convenient for small jobs but a dedicated landscape or masonry supply yard will typically beat their per-ton pricing on bulk bedding sand for larger driveways and patios.
          </p>
          <p>
            For premium and specialty polymeric sand brands — including the products we recommend below — a dedicated hardscape supplier or online ordering is often the more reliable route, since big-box inventory of higher-end brands varies significantly by region and season. If you're buying more than roughly 20-30 bags of any sand type, call ahead to confirm in-stock quantity before making the trip; polymeric sand in particular tends to sell out locally during peak spring and summer installation season.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more thing worth checking before you drive to any of these retailers: buy every bag for a single project from the same manufacturing lot or batch number if you can. Sand color and polymer ratio can shift subtly between production runs, and a visible seam between an older bag and a freshly opened one is one of the most common cosmetic complaints we hear from DIYers who bought their sand across two separate trips. If a store is short on stock, it's usually worth waiting a few days for a matching restock rather than mixing batches on a visible patio or walkway.
          </p>
