        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many bags of paver sand do I need per square foot?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For polymeric jointing sand at a standard 1/4-inch joint width and 1.5-inch depth, a 50-lb bag covers roughly 40-50 square feet, so most residential patios need one bag per 40-50 square feet plus about 15% extra for compaction settling and waste. Wider or deeper joints reduce coverage significantly — a 1-inch-wide flagstone joint can drop coverage to under 15 square feet per bag. For paver base/bedding sand at a standard 1-inch compacted layer, plan on roughly one 50-lb bag per 12 square feet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between paver sand and polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                "Paver sand" is a broad term that can refer to either bedding sand (the leveling layer pavers rest on, usually coarse concrete sand) or jointing sand (the material swept into the gaps between pavers). Polymeric sand is a specific type of jointing sand blended with a polymer binder that hardens when misted with water, locking joints in place and resisting weeds and washout. Plain bedding sand is never used in the joints of a finished installation, and polymeric sand should never be used as the base layer under pavers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is polymeric sand made of?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Polymeric sand is a blend of graded silica sand and a dry polymer binder, typically an acrylic or cross-linked polymer compound. When the swept-in sand is misted with water, the polymer dissolves briefly, coats the sand grains, and then cures into a semi-rigid matrix that binds the joint together — unlike plain sand, which has no binding agent and simply sits loose in the gap.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use regular sand or play sand instead of paver sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can use regular, unpolymerized sand for paver joints, but it isn't recommended for anything but a temporary or budget installation, since it washes out under rain and foot traffic within a season or two. Play sand specifically is the wrong choice — it's washed and rounded for softness and safety, not compaction, so it won't lock together the way angular paver bedding sand or concrete sand does. See our full <a href="/paver-sand-vs-play-sand/">Paver Sand vs. Play Sand comparison</a> for the complete breakdown.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can I buy polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Polymeric sand is widely stocked at Home Depot, Lowe's, and Menards in the US, at Bunnings Warehouse in Australia, and at most independent landscape supply and masonry yards. Big-box stores typically carry mid-range brands like Sakrete and Quikrete on the shelf, while premium brands such as Techniseal, Alliance Gator Maxx, and SEK Surebond are more often found through professional hardscape suppliers or ordered online.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a ton of paver sand cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A ton of sand covers roughly 80-100 square feet at a standard 3-inch depth, or about 240-300 square feet at a 1-inch bedding depth, though exact coverage varies with the sand's density and moisture content. As a rule of thumb, one cubic yard of sand weighs approximately 1.3-1.5 tons and covers 324 square feet at a 1-inch depth. For any project needing more than about a cubic yard of bedding sand, ordering bulk by the ton is almost always cheaper than buying dozens of bags.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What sand should I use to lay pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use coarse, angular concrete sand (sometimes called bedding sand or paving sand) as the 1-inch leveling layer that pavers are set into — never use fine play sand or beach sand for this layer, since rounded grains don't compact or lock together. After the pavers are laid and leveled, fill the joints between them with polymeric sand. For the full step-by-step installation process once your joints are sanded, see our <a href="/how-to-use-polymeric-sand/">How to Use Polymeric Sand</a> guide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does joint width and depth really change how much sand I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, significantly. Coverage per bag scales roughly with the total volume of the joints, which is a function of both width and depth. Doubling either the joint width or the joint depth roughly halves the square footage a single bag will cover. This is why a wide-joint flagstone patio can require two to three times more polymeric sand per square foot than a standard concrete paver patio with narrow joints.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
